            <ArticleTitle>Task Shifting in Dermatology: A Call to Action.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1179-1180</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1001/jamadermatol.2017.3342</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="Clinical Question" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Can task shifting be used to improve the delivery of dermatologic care in resource-poor settings worldwide?</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Bottom Line" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Task shifting is a means of redistributing available resources, whereby highly trained individuals train an available workforce to provide necessary care in low-resource settings. Limited evidence exists for task shifting in dermatology; however, studies from psychiatry demonstrate its efficacy. In the field of dermatology there is a need for high-quality evidence including randomized clinical trials to validate the implementation of task shifting in low-resource settings globally.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
